<section class="sec" id="s1">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">01</span><h2>What You Are Building</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">A ten-storey reinforced concrete residential building on a 60&prime; &times; 52&prime;
  footprint, with a grade beam level, a lift core shear wall, and a stair/lift machine room poking
  above the roof.</p>

  <p>The work splits into three phases, and they have to happen in order. Each one feeds the next,
  so a mistake in phase 1 shows up as garbage in phase 3.</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal"><span class="vtag">Phase 1</span><h4>Model</h4>
      <p>Grid, stories, sections, loads, combinations. Nothing is designed yet &mdash; you are just
      describing the building to the software.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb"><span class="vtag">Phase 2</span><h4>Analyse &amp; verify</h4>
      <p>Run it, then check three numbers by hand before you trust anything. This is the step
      everybody skips and it is the step that catches the errors.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-vio"><span class="vtag">Phase 3</span><h4>Detail &amp; draw</h4>
      <p>Pull the design output, choose bars, and draft the column schedule and the Beam AB
      long section in AutoCAD.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters"><span>&#9888;&#65039;</span><div>
    <b>One thing this page cannot do for you.</b> The C1/C2/C3 column layout comes from the plan
    in your assignment PDF, and that pattern is <em>not</em> symmetric &mdash; there is a C1 sitting
    at an interior position on the second row. Any layout invented here would be wrong. Section 04
    gives you the grid and a blank schedule to transcribe into; the positions themselves have to
    come off your drawing.
  </div></div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s2">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">02</span><h2>Grid and Story Data</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Three bays each way. The X bays are A&ndash;B&ndash;A; the Y bays are B&ndash;16&prime;&ndash;B,
  where that middle 16&prime; strip is the fixed 7&prime; + 9&prime; corridor from the plan and does
  <em>not</em> scale with your serial number.</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <div class="vtag" style="margin-bottom:.5rem">X grid &mdash; A + B + A</div>
      <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
        <tr><th>Axis</th><th class="n">Offset</th><th class="n">Cumulative</th></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">1</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="n" id="gx0">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">2</td><td class="n" id="gxa">21</td><td class="n" id="gx1">21</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">3</td><td class="n" id="gxb">18</td><td class="n" id="gx2">39</td></tr>
        <tr class="tot"><td class="k">4</td><td class="n" id="gxc">21</td><td class="n" id="gx3">60</td></tr>
      </table></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="vtag" style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Y grid &mdash; B + 16 + B</div>
      <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
        <tr><th>Axis</th><th class="n">Offset</th><th class="n">Cumulative</th></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">A</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="n">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">B</td><td class="n" id="gya">18</td><td class="n" id="gy1">18</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">C</td><td class="n">16</td><td class="n" id="gy2">34</td></tr>
        <tr class="tot"><td class="k">D</td><td class="n" id="gyc">18</td><td class="n" id="gy3">52</td></tr>
      </table></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="diagram-card">
    <h4>Framing plan &mdash; grid, column positions and Beam AB</h4>
    <svg id="planSvg" viewBox="0 0 460 420" class="calc-fig" style="max-width:460px" role="img"
         aria-label="Framing plan showing the three-by-three bay grid, sixteen column positions, and Beam AB along the top grid line"></svg>
    <p class="cap">Sixteen columns at the grid intersections. The highlighted node is the circled
    column for your schedule; Beam AB is the three-span line along the top.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Story data</h3>
  <p>Enter these in <span class="path">Edit &rsaquo; Stories and Grid Systems &rsaquo; Modify/Show Story Data</span>.
  Build them bottom-up. Only the ten typical floors are similar to each other &mdash; make Story 1 the
  master and set Stories 2&ndash;10 as similar to it, which is what lets you assign loads once.</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Level</th><th class="n">Height</th><th class="n">Elevation</th><th>Master / similar to</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Base</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="n">0</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Fixed restraint, all 16 columns</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">GB</td><td class="n">6&prime;-0&Prime;</td><td class="n">6&prime;-0&Prime;</td><td>Master</td><td>Grade beams only &mdash; no slab, no veranda</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Story 1</td><td class="n" id="stH">11&prime;-0&Prime;</td><td class="n" id="st1">17&prime;-0&Prime;</td><td>Master</td><td>Beam AB lives here</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Story 2&ndash;10</td><td class="n" id="stH2">11&prime;-0&Prime; each</td><td class="n" id="stR">up to 116&prime;-0&Prime;</td><td>Similar to Story 1</td><td>Typical floors</td></tr>
    <tr class="tot"><td class="k">Lift / stair top</td><td class="n">7&prime;-6&Prime;</td><td class="n" id="stL">123&prime;-6&Prime;</td><td>Master</td><td>Machine room over core only</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="core"><span>The two things people get wrong here</span>
    <p><b>1.</b> The GB level carries grade beams but no slab &mdash; if you draw a floor there, you
    add roughly <span id="ghostSlab">367</span> kip of dead load that does not exist.
    <b>2.</b> The lift/stair top is not a full floor. Draw it over the core footprint only,
    or your seismic weight and your base shear both come out high.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s3">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">03</span><h2>Materials and Sections</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Two concrete grades, one steel grade. The trap is that the slabs and stair are
  4000 psi while everything else is 4500 &mdash; define both materials before you define any section.</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Material</th><th class="n">f&prime;c / fy</th><th class="n">E (ksi)</th><th>Used by</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">CONC4500</td><td class="n">4500 psi</td><td class="n">3824</td><td>All columns, all beams, shear wall</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">CONC4000</td><td class="n">4000 psi</td><td class="n">3605</td><td>Floor slabs, stair slab</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Gr60</td><td class="n">60 ksi</td><td class="n">29000</td><td>All reinforcement</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">\( E_c = 57000\sqrt&#123;f'_c&#125; \) psi &mdash; 3824 ksi and 3605 ksi respectively. ETABS
  fills these in once you type f&prime;c, but check them; a wrong E changes every drift and period
  you report.</p>

  <p><span class="path">Define &rsaquo; Section Properties &rsaquo; Frame Sections</span> for the
  line elements, <span class="path">&rsaquo; Slab Sections</span> and
  <span class="path">&rsaquo; Wall Sections</span> for the shells.</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div>
      <div class="vtag" style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Frame sections &mdash; f&prime;c 4500, fy 60</div>
      <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
        <tr><th>Name</th><th class="n">b &times; h (in)</th><th class="n">Ag (in&sup2;)</th><th>Type</th></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">C1</td><td class="n">12 &times; 18</td><td class="n">216</td><td>Column</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">C2</td><td class="n">15 &times; 18</td><td class="n">270</td><td>Column</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">C3</td><td class="n">18 &times; 18</td><td class="n">324</td><td>Column</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">GB</td><td class="n">12 &times; 18</td><td class="n">216</td><td>Grade beam</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">FB</td><td class="n">12 &times; 20</td><td class="n">240</td><td>Floor beam</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">SB</td><td class="n">12 &times; 12</td><td class="n">144</td><td>Secondary beam</td></tr>
      </table></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="vtag" style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Shell sections</div>
      <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
        <tr><th>Name</th><th class="n">t</th><th class="n">f&prime;c</th><th>Modelling type</th></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">SLAB5</td><td class="n">5&Prime;</td><td class="n">4000</td><td>Shell &mdash; thin, membrane f11/f22 &times; 0.25 cracked</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">STAIR6</td><td class="n">6&Prime;</td><td class="n">4000</td><td>Shell &mdash; thin</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">SW10</td><td class="n">10&Prime;</td><td class="n">4500</td><td>Shell &mdash; thin, pier-labelled</td></tr>
      </table></div>
      <p class="small">Label the core walls as piers (<span class="path">Assign &rsaquo; Shell &rsaquo;
      Pier Label</span>) or ETABS reports wall forces element by element and you will not be able to
      design the core.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters"><span>&#128290;</span><div>
    <b>Property modifiers matter more than the section sizes.</b> For a dual system the slab in-plane
    stiffness is what drags load into the core. Use cracked-section modifiers per BNBC 2020 /
    ACI 318: beams 0.35Ig, columns 0.70Ig, walls 0.35Ig (uncracked 0.70Ig), slabs 0.25Ig.
    Set them at <span class="path">Assign &rsaquo; Frame &rsaquo; Property Modifiers</span>.
    Leave them at 1.0 and your period comes out short, your base shear high, and your drift
    unrealistically small.
  </div></div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s4">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">04</span><h2>The Column Layout Problem</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Sixteen columns, three section types, and a pattern that is not symmetric. This
  is the one part of the model you cannot infer &mdash; you have to read it off the plan.</p>

  <p>The grid gives you sixteen intersections: four X axes &times; four Y axes. What it does
  <em>not</em> give you is which of C1, C2 and C3 sits at each one. The plan in your assignment has
  a C1 at an interior position on the second row, which breaks any rule of thumb like
  &ldquo;corners are small, interiors are big.&rdquo; If you guess, you will get a plausible-looking
  model that is wrong in a way nobody catches until the column schedule.</p>

  <p>Transcribe the layout into this table from your PDF before you draw anything:</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Grid</th><th class="n">X (ft)</th><th class="n">Y (ft)</th><th>Section</th><th>Position</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">1-A</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Corner</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">2-A</td><td class="n" id="cxA">21</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Edge</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">3-A</td><td class="n" id="cxB">39</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Edge</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">4-A</td><td class="n" id="cxC">60</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Corner</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">1-B</td><td class="n">0</td><td class="n" id="cyA">18</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Edge</td></tr>
    <tr style="background:var(--adim)"><td class="k"><b>2-B</b></td><td class="n" id="cxA2">21</td><td class="n" id="cyA2">18</td><td class="k"><b>C3 &mdash; the circled one</b></td><td>Interior</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">3-B</td><td class="n" id="cxB2">39</td><td class="n" id="cyA3">18</td><td class="fillin">from plan &mdash; watch for the C1</td><td>Interior</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">4-B</td><td class="n" id="cxC2">60</td><td class="n" id="cyA4">18</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Edge</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">1-C &hellip; 4-C</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="n" id="cyB">34</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Edge / interior</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">1-D &hellip; 4-D</td><td class="n">&mdash;</td><td class="n" id="cyC">52</td><td class="fillin">from plan</td><td>Corner / edge</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="warnbox"><b>Verify the circled column before you commit.</b> Read as the C3 at
  X&nbsp;=&nbsp;<span id="ccx">21</span>&prime;, Y&nbsp;=&nbsp;<span id="ccy">18</span>&prime;
  &mdash; grid 2-B, the first interior intersection. That is the reading this page's Check&nbsp;1
  is built on. If the circle on your PDF is on a different node, the tributary area changes and so
  does every number in section 08. Confirm it against the original before you draft the schedule.</div>

  <p>Columns are drawn once and then replicated up the building. Draw the GB-to-Story-1 lift at
  the grid intersections, select all of them, and use
  <span class="path">Edit &rsaquo; Replicate &rsaquo; Story</span> to carry them to Story 10.
  Orientation matters for C1 and C2 since they are rectangular &mdash; the 18&Prime; dimension
  normally runs parallel to the beam that frames the weaker direction, so check the plan for which
  way each one is turned.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s5">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">05</span><h2>Loads and Where They Go</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Four load patterns carry gravity, and the mistake that ruins models is putting a
  load on the wrong kind of object &mdash; area loads onto slabs, line loads onto beams, and never
  both for the same physical thing.</p>

  <p><span class="path">Define &rsaquo; Load Patterns</span>. Self-weight multiplier is
  <b>1 on DEAD only</b> and <b>0 on everything else</b>. If you leave it at 1 on the superimposed
  pattern too, you count the whole concrete frame twice.</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Type</th><th class="n">Self-wt mult.</th><th>What it carries</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">DEAD</td><td>Dead</td><td class="n">1</td><td>Concrete self-weight, computed by ETABS</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">SDL</td><td>Super Dead</td><td class="n">0</td><td>FF 25 psf + PW 30 psf = 55 psf on slabs</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">WALL</td><td>Super Dead</td><td class="n">0</td><td>425 lb/ft line load on wall-supporting beams</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">LIVE</td><td>Live</td><td class="n">0</td><td>40 psf floors, 100 psf stair</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">EQX / EQY</td><td>Seismic</td><td class="n">0</td><td>BNBC 2020 auto-lateral</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">WX / WY</td><td>Wind</td><td class="n">0</td><td>BNBC 2020 auto-lateral</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Dead load accounting</h3>
  <p>The 5&Prime; slab weighs 62.5 psf on its own and ETABS adds that itself through DEAD. Your
  superimposed dead load is only the finish and partition allowance:</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Component</th><th class="n">Value</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Applied to</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Slab self-weight</td><td class="n">62.5 psf</td><td>DEAD (auto)</td><td>&mdash; do not enter</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Floor finish</td><td class="n">25 psf</td><td>SDL</td><td>All floor slabs</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Partition wall allowance</td><td class="n">30 psf</td><td>SDL</td><td>All floor slabs</td></tr>
    <tr class="tot"><td class="k">Total slab dead load</td><td class="n">117.5 psf</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="pathline">Assign &rsaquo; Shell Loads &rsaquo; Uniform &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; SDL, 0.055 ksf, Gravity direction</div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Wall load &mdash; the 425 lb/ft line</h3>
  <p>This is a 5&Prime; brick wall carried on a beam. It goes on <em>beams</em>, not slabs, and only
  on the beams that actually have a wall over them: the full perimeter at every floor, plus the
  interior partition lines shown on your architectural plan.</p>

  <div class="pathline">Assign &rsaquo; Frame Loads &rsaquo; Distributed &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; WALL, 0.425 kip/ft, Gravity</div>

  <div class="analogy"><span class="an-icon">&#129513;</span><div>
    A quick sanity check on the 425 figure: a 5&Prime; brick wall at about 120 pcf, 10&prime; clear
    height, is \(0.417 \times 10 \times 120 \approx 500\) lb/ft gross. Take out the door and window
    openings and 425 lb/ft is about right for a residential floor. If your plan has a solid
    party wall with no openings, that line deserves more.
  </div></div>

  <div class="warnbox"><b>Do not put the wall load on the perimeter at the GB level unless there
  is a plinth wall there.</b> And do not put it on the roof. Both are easy to catch afterwards
  because the total will not match: your WALL pattern base reaction should be roughly
  <span id="wallTot">1462</span> kip for ten floors at the assumed wall lengths.</div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Live load</h3>
  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Area</th><th class="n">Live load</th><th>Note</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">All floor slabs</td><td class="n">40 psf</td><td>Residential occupancy</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Stair slab</td><td class="n">100 psf</td><td>Egress &mdash; applies to the 6&Prime; stair shell</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Roof</td><td class="n">40 psf</td><td>Unless your brief says otherwise</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">Live load reduction: ETABS will not apply it unless you switch it on, and for a
  ten-storey column it makes a real difference &mdash; see Check&nbsp;1, where it takes the
  accumulated live load from <span id="llFull">133</span> kip down to <span id="llRed">53</span> kip.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s6">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">06</span><h2>The Four Load Combinations</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Define exactly these four at <span class="path">Define &rsaquo; Load Combinations</span>.
  Not ETABS's auto-generated set &mdash; these specific four, with these names.</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Name</th><th>Definition</th><th>Type</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">UFL</td><td>1.0 DL + 1.0 LL</td><td>Service</td><td>Deflection, drift, unfactored reactions</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">FDL</td><td>1.2 DL + 1.6 LL</td><td>Strength</td><td>Governing gravity case</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">FDLEQy</td><td>0.9 DL + 1.2 LL + 1.32 EQy</td><td>Strength</td><td>Gravity + seismic, Y direction</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">FDLWx</td><td>0.9 DL + 1.2 LL + 1.2 Wx</td><td>Strength</td><td>Gravity + wind, X direction</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="why-matters"><span>&#128200;</span><div>
    <b>&ldquo;DL&rdquo; here means all three dead patterns.</b> When you build these combinations,
    DL expands to DEAD + SDL + WALL. So FDL is really
    <em>1.2(DEAD) + 1.2(SDL) + 1.2(WALL) + 1.6(LIVE)</em> &mdash; four rows in the combination
    dialog, not two. Leaving WALL out of the combinations is the single most common reason a
    student's beam moments come out low against a hand check.
  </div></div>

  <p>Two of these are one-directional by design. FDLEQy only covers seismic in Y and FDLWx only
  covers wind in X, which is what the brief asks for. Be aware that means the combination set is
  not a complete design envelope &mdash; a real design needs EQx and Wy too, plus the negative
  senses of each. Say so on your sheet rather than quietly implying the four are sufficient.</p>

  <p>For the Beam AB envelope in section 11, take the worst of FDL, FDLEQy and FDLWx at each
  station. Under the lateral combinations the support moments grow and can reverse sign, which is
  exactly why the bottom steel has to be continued into the supports.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s7">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">07</span><h2>Seismic and Wind Parameters</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Both are auto-lateral loads in ETABS. Enter the code parameters and let it build
  the storey force distribution &mdash; then check the total against section 10.</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-rust"><span class="vtag">Seismic &mdash; BNBC 2020</span>
      <h4>Dhaka, Zone 2</h4>
      <p>
        Z = 0.20 &middot; I = 1.0 &middot; Site Class SD<br>
        S = 1.5, T<sub>B</sub> = 0.2 s, T<sub>C</sub> = 0.8 s, T<sub>D</sub> = 2.0 s<br>
        R = choose for your system, state it<br>
        &eta; = 1.0 at 5% damping
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-blu"><span class="vtag">Wind &mdash; BNBC 2020</span>
      <h4>Dhaka, urban</h4>
      <p>
        V = 147 mph (65.7 m/s), 3-second gust<br>
        Exposure A (urban) &middot; I = 1.0<br>
        K<sub>d</sub> = 0.85, K<sub>zt</sub> = 1.0, G = 0.85<br>
        C<sub>p</sub> = +0.8 windward, &minus;0.5 leeward
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="warnbox"><b>Two values to confirm rather than take from this page.</b> Z = 0.20 and
  the Site Class SD spectral parameters both come from BNBC 2020 Part 6 Chapter 2, and the site
  class in particular depends on the soil report for your site, not on the city. Look them up and
  cite the table on your sheet. If your site is on soft Dhaka alluvium, SE is plausible and it
  changes S from 1.5 to 2.4 &mdash; a 60% jump in base shear.</div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Choosing R</h3>
  <p>You have a dual system: moment frames plus a lift-core shear wall. The R value depends on the
  detailing level you commit to, and it is a factor-of-1.3 swing in your design forces, so it is
  not a throwaway choice:</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>System</th><th class="n">R</th><th class="n">Resulting V</th><th>Detailing burden</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Dual &mdash; intermediate frame + ordinary RC wall</td><td class="n">5.5</td><td class="n" id="vR55">620 k</td><td>Moderate; realistic for Zone 2</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Dual &mdash; special frame + special RC wall</td><td class="n">7.0</td><td class="n" id="vR70">487 k</td><td>Full seismic detailing throughout</td></tr>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">Both rows assume the frames can independently resist at least 25% of the base
  shear, which is the defining condition for a dual system. Check that in your model by running the
  frames without the wall &mdash; if they cannot, it is a wall system and R drops again.</p>

  <div class="core"><span>State your assumption</span>
    <p>Whichever R you pick, write it on the sheet with a one-line reason. &ldquo;R = 5.5, dual
    system with intermediate RC moment frames and ordinary RC shear walls, BNBC 2020 Table 6.2.19&rdquo;
    is a defensible sentence. An R with no justification is the first thing a reviewer circles.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s8">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">08</span><h2>Check 1 &mdash; Column Axial Load</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">The circled C3 at grid 2-B, at ground floor, where it is carrying everything above
  it. This is the check that tells you whether your load assignment is right, because it depends on
  every pattern at once.</p>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Worked check</span><h4>Factored axial load on C3 at grid 2-B</h4></div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Tributary area.</strong> Half a bay each way.
        X: <span id="t1x">10.5 + 9.0 = 19.5</span> ft. Y: <span id="t1y">9.0 + 8.0 = 17.0</span> ft.
        \( A_T = \) <span id="t1a">331.5</span> ft&sup2; per floor.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Slab load into the column.</strong> At 117.5 psf dead
        and 40 psf live: DL = <span id="t1sd">39.0</span> kip, LL = <span id="t1sl">13.3</span> kip
        per floor.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Beams framing in.</strong> The FB 12&times;20 stem below
        the slab is 12&Prime; &times; 15&Prime; = 0.1875 kip/ft. Tributary beam length is
        <span id="t1bl">36.5</span> ft, giving <span id="t1bd">6.8</span> kip per floor.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Column self-weight.</strong> 18&times;18 over
        <span id="t1ch">11</span> ft = <span id="t1cd">3.7</span> kip per storey.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Wall load.</strong> This is the judgement call. If both
        grid lines through 2-B carry partition walls, that is <span id="t1w2">15.5</span> kip per
        floor; if only one does, <span id="t1w1">8.3</span> kip. Read it off the plan &mdash; it
        swings the answer by 11%.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>6</b><div><strong>Live load reduction.</strong> A column supporting ten
        floors gets the full reduction.
        \( L = L_o(0.25 + 15/\sqrt&#123;K_&#123;LL&#125;A_T&#125;) \) with \(K_&#123;LL&#125; = 4\)
        and \(A_T = \) <span id="t1at">3315</span> ft&sup2; gives
        <span id="t1lr">16.0</span> psf, at the \(0.4L_o\) floor.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>7</b><div><strong>Accumulate and factor.</strong>
        \( P_u = 1.2D + 1.6L \).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-results" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="res"><span>Dead load, 10 floors (walls both lines)</span><b id="r1d2">650 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>Dead load, 10 floors (wall one line)</span><b id="r1d1">578 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>Reduced live load, 10 floors</span><b id="r1l">53 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>P<sub>u</sub> &mdash; walls both lines</span><b id="r1u2">865 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>P<sub>u</sub> &mdash; wall one line</span><b id="r1u1">778 kip</b></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Is 18 &times; 18 enough?</h3>
  <p>For a tied column the code caps the pure-axial capacity at
  \( \phi P_&#123;n,max&#125; = 0.80\phi[0.85f'_c(A_g - A_&#123;st&#125;) + f_y A_&#123;st&#125;] \)
  with \(\phi = 0.65\). At \(A_g\) = 324 in&sup2;:</p>

  <div class="calc-results">
    <div class="res"><span>8-#8 &nbsp;(&rho; = 1.95%)</span><b id="cap1">829 kip</b></div>
    <div class="res"><span>8-#9 &nbsp;(&rho; = 2.47%)</span><b id="cap2">878 kip</b></div>
    <div class="res"><span>12-#8 (&rho; = 2.93%)</span><b id="cap3">921 kip</b></div>
  </div>
  <div id="v1" style="margin-top:.6rem"></div>

  <div class="warnbox"><b>This column is tight, and that is the real finding.</b> Pure axial
  capacity at 2% steel is about <span id="capNote">829</span> kip against a demand between
  <span id="demLo">778</span> and <span id="demHi">865</span> kip &mdash; and that is before any
  moment from the lateral combinations, which a corner or interior column always picks up.
  Expect the ETABS design to want 2.5&ndash;3% steel here, or a bigger section in the lower
  storeys. If ETABS comes back with 1% at ground floor, something is not being applied.</div>

  <div class="core"><span>How to read this against ETABS</span>
    <p>Pull the axial force at the base of the 2-B column under FDL. It should land in the
    <span id="bandLo">780</span>&ndash;<span id="bandHi">870</span> kip band. Over that band means
    you have doubled a load somewhere &mdash; usually self-weight on two patterns, or a slab drawn
    at the GB level. Well under it means a pattern is not reaching the slab, or live load reduction
    is set more aggressively than the hand calc.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s9">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">09</span><h2>Check 2 &mdash; Beam AB Moments</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Beam AB runs along the top grid line, Y = <span id="abY">52</span>&prime;, at Story 1.
  Three continuous spans of <span id="abSpans">21&prime; &ndash; 18&prime; &ndash; 21&prime;</span>,
  section FB 12&times;20.</p>

  <p>The ACI approximate moment coefficients are legitimate here, and worth using because they give
  you an independent number that owes nothing to your model. Check the conditions first &mdash; all
  four hold:</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Condition</th><th>This beam</th><th class="n">OK?</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Two or more spans</td><td>Three</td><td class="n">&check;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Adjacent spans differ by &le; 20%</td><td id="c2span">21 vs 18 &rarr; 14.3%</td><td class="n">&check;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Loads uniformly distributed</td><td>Slab + wall, both uniform</td><td class="n">&check;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Unfactored L/D &le; 3</td><td id="c2ld">0.19</td><td class="n">&check;</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Worked check</span><h4>Load on the beam, then the moments</h4></div>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Slab load onto an edge beam.</strong> AB is on the
        perimeter, so slab arrives from one side only. The panel is
        <span id="c2panel">18&prime; &times; 21&prime;</span> and the load pattern is trapezoidal,
        not a plain half-bay strip. The equivalent uniform load on the long beam is
        \( w_&#123;eq&#125; = \dfrac&#123;wS&#125;&#123;2&#125;\left(1 - \dfrac&#123;1&#125;&#123;3m^2&#125;\right) \),
        \( m = L/S \).</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>End span, 21&prime;.</strong> \(m = \) <span id="c2m">1.167</span>,
        so \(w_&#123;eq&#125;\) = <span id="c2wd">0.799</span> k/ft dead and
        <span id="c2wl">0.272</span> k/ft live from the slab.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Add the beam and the wall.</strong> Stem 0.1875 k/ft,
        brick wall 0.425 k/ft. Total D = <span id="c2D">1.411</span> k/ft, L =
        <span id="c2L">0.272</span> k/ft.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Factor.</strong>
        \(w_u = 1.2D + 1.6L = \) <span id="c2wu">2.128</span> k/ft on the end spans,
        <span id="c2wum">1.965</span> k/ft on the middle span.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Clear spans.</strong> With 18&Prime; columns,
        \(l_n\) = <span id="c2lne">19.50</span> ft (end) and <span id="c2lnm">16.50</span> ft (middle);
        the interior support uses the average, <span id="c2lna">18.00</span> ft.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="tw" style="margin-top:1rem"><table class="dt">
      <tr><th>Location</th><th>Coefficient</th><th class="n">M<sub>u</sub> (k-ft)</th><th class="n">A<sub>s</sub> req (in&sup2;)</th><th>Bars</th></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">&minus;M exterior support</td><td>w<sub>u</sub>l<sub>n</sub>&sup2;/16</td><td class="n" id="m1">50.6</td><td class="n" id="a1">0.70</td><td id="b1">2-#6</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">+M end span (21&prime;)</td><td>w<sub>u</sub>l<sub>n</sub>&sup2;/14</td><td class="n" id="m2">57.8</td><td class="n" id="a2">0.76</td><td id="b2">2-#6</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">&minus;M first interior support</td><td>w<sub>u</sub>l<sub>n</sub>&sup2;/10</td><td class="n" id="m3">69.0</td><td class="n" id="a3">0.91</td><td id="b3">2-#7</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">+M middle span (18&prime;)</td><td>w<sub>u</sub>l<sub>n</sub>&sup2;/16</td><td class="n" id="m4">33.4</td><td class="n" id="a4">0.70</td><td id="b4">2-#6</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <p class="small">\(A_s\) from \(R_n = M_u/\phi bd^2\) with \(d\) = 17.5&Prime;, then
    \(\rho = \dfrac&#123;0.85f'_c&#125;&#123;f_y&#125;\left(1-\sqrt&#123;1-\dfrac&#123;2R_n&#125;&#123;0.85f'_c&#125;&#125;\right)\).
    Minimum steel \(\rho_&#123;min&#125; = 3\sqrt&#123;f'_c&#125;/f_y = 0.00335\) governs three of the
    four locations &mdash; \(A_&#123;s,min&#125;\) = <span id="asmin">0.70</span> in&sup2;.</p>

    <div class="ans" style="margin-top:.8rem"><strong>The number to check against ETABS:</strong>
    positive moment at the 21&prime; end span &asymp; <span id="ansM">58</span> k-ft under FDL,
    negative at the interior supports &asymp; <span id="ansM2">69</span> k-ft. ETABS solves the
    frame properly rather than with coefficients, so expect it to land within about 10&ndash;15%
    &mdash; usually a little lower at midspan and a little higher at the supports.</div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Shear</h3>
  <p>\( \phi V_c = 0.75 \times 2\sqrt&#123;f'_c&#125;b_wd = \) <span id="phiVc">21.1</span> kip,
  against a maximum \(V_u\) of <span id="vumax">23.9</span> kip at the face of the first interior
  support. Stirrups are needed but the demand is small &mdash; \(V_s\) required is only
  <span id="vsreq">3.6</span> kip, so the maximum spacing \(d/2\) governs everywhere.</p>
  <div class="ans">#3 double-leg stirrups @ <span id="sMax">8</span>&Prime; c/c throughout, tightened
  to 4&Prime; over 2h = 40&Prime; from each support face for seismic detailing.</div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s10">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">10</span><h2>Check 3 &mdash; Base Shear</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">The global check. If your seismic weight is wrong, everything lateral is wrong,
  and this catches it in five minutes.</p>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Worked check</span><h4>Seismic weight and V = S<sub>a</sub>W</h4></div>
    <p class="prob">Floor area is <span id="s3area">3120</span> ft&sup2;. Build the weight up
    component by component for one typical floor:</p>
    <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
      <tr><th>Component</th><th>Basis</th><th class="n">kip / floor</th></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">Slab + finishes</td><td>117.5 psf over the plan area</td><td class="n" id="w1">366.6</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">Beams</td><td>0.1875 k/ft stem, all grid lines</td><td class="n" id="w2">84.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">Columns</td><td>16 columns, average 15&times;18</td><td class="n" id="w3">49.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">Brick walls</td><td>0.425 k/ft, perimeter + partitions</td><td class="n" id="w4">146.2</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="k">Lift core wall</td><td>10&Prime; wall, ~26 ft net length</td><td class="n" id="w5">35.8</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td class="k">W per floor</td><td id="wpsf">219 psf</td><td class="n" id="w6">682</td></tr>
      <tr class="tot"><td class="k">W total</td><td id="wnst">10 floors</td><td class="n" id="w7">6,821</td></tr>
    </table></div>

    <div class="steps" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Period.</strong> \(T = C_t h_n^m\) with
        \(h_n\) = <span id="s3hn">116</span> ft = <span id="s3hnm">35.4</span> m. For a dual system
        (\(C_t\) = 0.0488, m = 0.75), \(T\) = <span id="s3T">0.708</span> s.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Spectrum.</strong> That falls between \(T_B\) = 0.2 s
        and \(T_C\) = 0.8 s, so \(C_s = 2.5S\eta\) = <span id="s3Cs">3.750</span>.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Design acceleration.</strong>
        \( S_a = \dfrac&#123;2&#125;&#123;3&#125;\dfrac&#123;ZI&#125;&#123;R&#125;C_s \).</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Base shear.</strong> \(V = S_aW\).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-results" style="margin-top:.8rem">
      <div class="res"><span>V &mdash; R = 5.5, T = 0.71 s</span><b id="s3v1">620 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>V &mdash; R = 7.0, T = 0.71 s</span><b id="s3v2">487 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>V &mdash; R = 5.5, longer T = 1.15 s</span><b id="s3v3">430 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>Wind, X direction (on the <span id="s3fx">52</span>&prime; face)</span><b id="s3wx">282 kip</b></div>
      <div class="res"><span>Wind, Y direction (on the <span id="s3fy">60</span>&prime; face)</span><b id="s3wy">326 kip</b></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="core"><span>What this tells you</span>
    <p>Seismic base shear should land somewhere around
    <span id="s3band">5&ndash;9%</span> of W, i.e. roughly
    <span id="s3lo">340</span>&ndash;<span id="s3hi">620</span> kip depending on the R and the
    period you end up with. Wind is <span id="s3windcmp">smaller but not negligible</span> at
    <span id="s3wmax">326</span> kip. Compare both against your ETABS auto-lateral totals under
    <span class="path">Display &rsaquo; Load Cases &rsaquo; Base Reactions</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters"><span>&#128336;</span><div>
    <b>Your period will not match the code formula, and that is normal.</b> \(C_th_n^m\) is a
    deliberately short empirical estimate; the ETABS modal period from a cracked-section model will
    typically be 1.3&ndash;1.6&times; longer. The code caps how much of that you may use
    (\(T \le C_uT_a\)), so the design base shear stays near the empirical value even when the model
    is softer. Report both periods and say which one drove the design.
  </div></div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s11">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">11</span><h2>Beam AB &mdash; Detailing and Curtailment</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">This is what the AutoCAD long section has to show: where the bars are, where they
  stop, and why they stop there.</p>

  <p>Pull the moment envelope from ETABS first. Take the worst of FDL, FDLEQy and FDLWx at every
  station along the beam, not just the FDL diagram &mdash; under the lateral combinations the
  support moments grow and the end-span support moment can reverse.</p>

  <div class="pathline">Display &rsaquo; Force/Stress Diagrams &rsaquo; Frame/Pier/Spandrel Forces &nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp; Moment 3-3, envelope, show values</div>

  <div class="diagram-card">
    <h4>Beam AB long section &mdash; bar arrangement and cut-off points</h4>
    <svg id="beamSvg" viewBox="0 0 640 250" class="calc-fig" style="max-width:640px" role="img"
         aria-label="Long section of the three-span beam AB showing top bars over supports, bottom bars in the spans, and the cut-off dimensions"></svg>
    <p class="cap">Top steel over the supports, bottom steel in the spans, and the cut-off distances
    measured from each support face. Dimensions update with your span lengths.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Curtailment rules that set those dimensions</h3>
  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Bar</th><th>Rule</th><th class="n">This beam</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Top, interior support</td><td>Extend l<sub>n</sub>/4 past the face each side</td><td class="n" id="cut1">4&prime;-11&Prime;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Top, into the 18&prime; span</td><td>l<sub>n</sub>/4 of the shorter span</td><td class="n" id="cut2">4&prime;-2&Prime;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Top, exterior support</td><td>l<sub>n</sub>/5, hooked into the column</td><td class="n" id="cut3">3&prime;-11&Prime;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Bottom, into support</td><td>At least &frac14; of +A<sub>s</sub> continuous, 6&Prime; min into the support</td><td class="n">2-#6 through</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Development, #7 top</td><td>l<sub>d</sub>, top-bar factor &psi;<sub>t</sub> = 1.3</td><td class="n" id="ld7">4&prime;-3&Prime;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Development, #6 bottom</td><td>l<sub>d</sub>, &psi;<sub>s</sub> = 0.8</td><td class="n" id="ld6">1&prime;-9&Prime;</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">Standard hook, #7</td><td>l<sub>dh</sub>, 90&deg; hook</td><td class="n" id="ldh7">1&prime;-1&Prime;</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="warnbox"><b>Check the cut-off against development length, not just against l<sub>n</sub>/4.</b>
  The #7 top bar needs <span id="ld7b">4&prime;-3&Prime;</span> of embedment from the point of
  maximum stress, and l<sub>n</sub>/4 here is <span id="cut1b">4&prime;-11&Prime;</span>. Those are
  close enough that a slightly shorter span or a larger bar flips which one governs. Take the
  larger of the two and round up to the next 3&Prime;.</div>

  <div class="core"><span>Also required by ACI at every cut-off</span>
    <p>Extend every bar a distance \(d\) or \(12d_b\) beyond the point where it is no longer needed
    for flexure &mdash; here that is 17.5&Prime; and 10.5&Prime; for a #7, so 17.5&Prime; governs.
    And at least one third of the negative steel must run past the inflection point by \(d\),
    \(12d_b\), or \(l_n/16\), whichever is greatest. Show both on the drawing; markers are what
    the checker looks for.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s12">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">12</span><h2>The AutoCAD Drawing Set</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">Four drawings, and each one has a specific job. Draw them at a real scale in
  model space and plot through a layout &mdash; do not draw to fit the paper.</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-teal"><span class="vtag">Drawing 1</span><h4>Framing plan</h4>
      <p>Grid with dimensions, all sixteen columns with their section marks, beam marks on every
      line, slab panel callouts. Scale 1/8&Prime; = 1&prime;-0&Prime;. This is the drawing that
      proves your column layout matches the architectural plan.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb"><span class="vtag">Drawing 2</span><h4>Column schedule</h4>
      <p>The circled column, storey by storey: section size, bar count and size, tie size and
      spacing, and the splice location. One row per storey group. Include the load it was designed
      for so the numbers are traceable.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-vio"><span class="vtag">Drawing 3</span><h4>Beam AB long section</h4>
      <p>Three spans at 1/4&Prime; = 1&prime;-0&Prime; horizontal. Top and bottom bars with
      cut-off dimensions, stirrup zones and spacings, support faces marked. Add the moment envelope
      above it at the same horizontal scale so the steel visibly follows the moment.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-blu"><span class="vtag">Drawing 4</span><h4>Sections</h4>
      <p>Cut through the beam at midspan and at a support, 1&Prime; = 1&prime;-0&Prime;. Two
      sections, because the bar arrangement is different at each &mdash; that difference is the
      whole point of the drawing.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Layer discipline</h3>
  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Colour</th><th>Lineweight</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">S-GRID</td><td>Grey 8</td><td class="n">0.09</td><td>Grid lines, bubbles</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">S-CONC</td><td>Cyan 4</td><td class="n">0.35</td><td>Concrete outlines</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">S-REBAR</td><td>Red 1</td><td class="n">0.50</td><td>Main bars &mdash; heaviest line on the sheet</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">S-STIRRUP</td><td>Green 3</td><td class="n">0.25</td><td>Stirrups, ties</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">S-DIM</td><td>Yellow 2</td><td class="n">0.13</td><td>Dimensions, leaders</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k">S-TEXT</td><td>White 7</td><td class="n">0.18</td><td>Notes, bar callouts</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="why-matters"><span>&#128207;</span><div>
    <b>Reinforcement is the subject of these drawings, so it gets the heaviest line.</b> The most
    common presentation failure is a beam section where the concrete outline is bolder than the
    bars, which reads as a drawing about a rectangle. Set S-REBAR to 0.50 mm and let everything
    else sit behind it.
  </div></div>

  <p>Annotate every bar as <em>count &ndash; size &ndash; length</em>, for example
  <span class="path">2-#7 &times; 12&prime;-6&Prime;</span>, and give each a bar mark that matches
  a bar bending schedule on the same sheet. A drawing with bars but no schedule cannot be
  fabricated from.</p>
</section>

<section class="sec" id="s13">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">13</span><h2>When ETABS Disagrees With You</h2></div>
  <p class="lead">A 15% gap between hand check and model is normal. Beyond that, it is almost
  always one of these six things, roughly in order of how often it happens.</p>

  <div class="tw"><table class="dt">
    <tr><th class="n">#</th><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
    <tr><td class="n">1</td><td>Everything is ~50% heavy</td><td>Self-weight multiplier left at 1 on SDL as well as DEAD</td><td>Set SDL and WALL multipliers to 0</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="n">2</td><td>Base reaction high by ~360 kip</td><td>A slab drawn at the GB level</td><td>Delete it &mdash; GB carries beams only</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="k n">3</td><td>Beam moments low by ~25%</td><td>WALL pattern missing from the combinations</td><td>DL means DEAD + SDL + WALL, three rows each</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="n">4</td><td>Column axial low</td><td>Slab not meshed, so load never reaches the beams</td><td>Auto-mesh the floor, or check the load path</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="n">5</td><td>Base shear high, drift tiny</td><td>Property modifiers left at 1.0</td><td>Apply cracked-section modifiers</td></tr>
    <tr><td class="n">6</td><td>Wall forces unusable</td><td>Core walls not pier-labelled</td><td>Assign pier labels to the whole core</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <div class="core"><span>The order to check things in</span>
    <p>Always start with the total base reaction under DEAD alone and compare it to
    <span id="tbLo">6800</span> kip &plusmn; 10% for a building this size. That one number catches
    causes 1, 2 and 4 immediately. Only once the gravity total is right does it make sense to look
    at individual members.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="font-family:'Space Mono',monospace;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--txt);margin:1.6rem 0 .3rem">Quick reference</h3>
  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>Plan</span><span id="q1">60&prime; &times; 52&prime;</span></div>
    <div><span>Roof elevation</span><span id="q2">116&prime;-0&Prime;</span></div>
    <div><span>Slab dead load</span><span>117.5 psf</span></div>
    <div><span>Floor live load</span><span>40 psf</span></div>
    <div><span>Wall line load</span><span>0.425 k/ft</span></div>
    <div><span>Seismic weight</span><span id="q3">6,821 kip</span></div>
    <div><span>C3 tributary</span><span id="q4">331.5 ft&sup2;</span></div>
    <div><span>C3 P<sub>u</sub> band</span><span id="q5">780&ndash;870 kip</span></div>
    <div><span>Beam AB +M end span</span><span id="q6">57.8 k-ft</span></div>
    <div><span>Beam AB &minus;M interior</span><span id="q7">69.0 k-ft</span></div>
    <div><span>Seismic base shear</span><span id="q8">340&ndash;620 kip</span></div>
    <div><span>Wind base shear</span><span id="q9">282&ndash;326 kip</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters"><span>&#9997;&#65039;</span><div>
    <b>State your assumptions on the sheet.</b> Four of them matter enough to write down: the R
    value and why; the site class and where it came from; whether the wall load sits on one or both
    grid lines through the circled column; and the fact that the four required combinations cover
    only EQy and Wx, so they are not a full design envelope. Assumptions that are written down are
    engineering judgement. The same assumptions left implicit are just gaps.
  </div></div>
</section>

<nav class="chapter-nav">
  <a href="/civil">&larr; Civil Engineering</a>
  <span class="next">Build it, check it, then draw it.</span>
</nav>

<script is:inline>
(function(){
  const g = id => document.getElementById(id);
  const MID_Y = 16, GB = 6, LIFT = 7.5;          /* fixed by the plan */
  const wc = 0.150, tsl = 5/12;
  const FF = 0.025, PW = 0.030, LLo = 0.040, WALL = 0.425;
  const DLslab = tsl*wc + FF + PW;               /* 0.1175 ksf */
  const fbStem = (12*15/144)*wc;                 /* 0.1875 k/ft */
  const fc = 4.5, fy = 60;

  const f1 = n => n.toFixed(1), f2 = n => n.toFixed(2), f3 = n => n.toFixed(3);
  const r0 = n => Math.round(n).toLocaleString();
  const set = (id,v) => { const e = g(id); if(e) e.innerHTML = v; };
  /* decimal feet -> 12'-3" , rounding inches to the nearest whole inch */
  function fi(x){
    let ft = Math.floor(x), inch = Math.round((x-ft)*12);
    if(inch === 12){ ft++; inch = 0; }
    return ft + "&prime;-" + inch + "&Prime;";
  }
  const BARS = [["2-#6",0.88],["2-#7",1.20],["3-#6",1.32],["2-#8",1.58],["3-#7",1.80],["4-#7",2.40]];
  const pick = As => (BARS.find(b => b[1] >= As) || BARS[BARS.length-1])[0];

  /* equivalent UDL delivered to the LONG beam of a two-way panel S x L */
  function weq(w,S,L){ const m = L/S; return (w*S/2)*(1 - 1/(3*m*m)); }

  /* As from Mu (k-ft) for a singly reinforced rectangular section */
  function AsReq(Mkft,b,d){
    const Rn = Mkft*12/(0.9*b*d*d);
    const inner = 1 - 2*Rn/(0.85*fc);
    if(inner < 0) return NaN;
    return (0.85*fc/fy)*(1-Math.sqrt(inner))*b*d;
  }

  function recalc(){
    const A = +g('pA').value || 21, B = +g('pB').value || 18;
    const C = +g('pC').value || 11, N = Math.max(1, Math.round(+g('pN').value || 10));
    const Lx = 2*A + B, Ly = 2*B + MID_Y;
    const X = [0, A, A+B, Lx], Y = [0, B, B+MID_Y, Ly];
    const area = Lx*Ly, hn = GB + N*C;

    /* ---------- 02 grid + stories ---------- */
    set('gxa',f1(A)); set('gxb',f1(B)); set('gxc',f1(A));
    X.forEach((v,i)=>set('gx'+i,f1(v)));
    set('gya',f1(B)); set('gyc',f1(B));
    for(let i=1;i<4;i++) set('gy'+i,f1(Y[i]));
    set('stH',fi(C)); set('st1',fi(GB+C)); set('stH2',fi(C)+" each");
    set('stR',"up to "+fi(hn)); set('stL',fi(hn+LIFT));
    set('ghostSlab',r0(area*DLslab));

    /* ---------- 04 column grid coords ---------- */
    ['cxA','cxA2'].forEach(i=>set(i,f1(X[1]))); ['cxB','cxB2'].forEach(i=>set(i,f1(X[2])));
    ['cxC','cxC2'].forEach(i=>set(i,f1(X[3])));
    ['cyA','cyA2','cyA3','cyA4'].forEach(i=>set(i,f1(Y[1])));
    set('cyB',f1(Y[2])); set('cyC',f1(Y[3]));
    set('ccx',f1(X[1])); set('ccy',f1(Y[1]));

    /* ---------- 08 column axial ---------- */
    const tX = (X[1]-X[0])/2 + (X[2]-X[1])/2, tY = (Y[1]-Y[0])/2 + (Y[2]-Y[1])/2;
    const AT = tX*tY, beamLen = tX + tY;
    const slabD = AT*DLslab, slabL = AT*LLo;
    const beamD = beamLen*fbStem, colD = (324/144)*wc*C;
    const wall2 = beamLen*WALL, wall1 = tX*WALL;
    const ATtot = N*AT;
    let Lred = LLo*1000*(0.25 + 15/Math.sqrt(4*ATtot));
    Lred = Math.max(Lred, 0.4*LLo*1000);
    const LLr = N*AT*Lred/1000, LLfull = N*slabL;
    const D2 = N*(slabD+beamD+wall2+colD), D1 = N*(slabD+beamD+wall1+colD);
    const Pu2 = 1.2*D2 + 1.6*LLr, Pu1 = 1.2*D1 + 1.6*LLr;

    set('t1x',f1((X[1]-X[0])/2)+" + "+f1((X[2]-X[1])/2)+" = "+f1(tX));
    set('t1y',f1((Y[1]-Y[0])/2)+" + "+f1((Y[2]-Y[1])/2)+" = "+f1(tY));
    set('t1a',f1(AT)); set('t1sd',f1(slabD)); set('t1sl',f1(slabL));
    set('t1bl',f1(beamLen)); set('t1bd',f1(beamD)); set('t1ch',f1(C)); set('t1cd',f1(colD));
    set('t1w2',f1(wall2)); set('t1w1',f1(wall1));
    set('t1at',r0(ATtot)); set('t1lr',f1(Lred));
    set('r1d2',r0(D2)+" kip"); set('r1d1',r0(D1)+" kip"); set('r1l',r0(LLr)+" kip");
    set('r1u2',r0(Pu2)+" kip"); set('r1u1',r0(Pu1)+" kip");
    set('llFull',r0(LLfull)); set('llRed',r0(LLr));

    const caps = [["8-#8",6.32],["8-#9",8.00],["12-#8",9.48]].map(b =>
      0.65*0.80*(0.85*fc*(324-b[1]) + fy*b[1]));
    caps.forEach((c,i)=>set('cap'+(i+1), r0(c)+" kip"));
    set('capNote',r0(caps[0])); set('demLo',r0(Pu1)); set('demHi',r0(Pu2));
    set('bandLo',r0(Math.round(Pu1/10)*10)); set('bandHi',r0(Math.round(Pu2/10)*10));
    const ok = caps[0] >= Pu2;
    set('v1','<div class="verdict '+(ok?'v-pass':'v-fail')+'">'+
      '<span>18&times;18 with 8-#8 vs P<sub>u</sub> = '+r0(Pu2)+' kip (walls both lines)</span>'+
      '<b>'+(ok?'OK':'NOT ENOUGH — go to 8-#9 or larger')+'</b></div>');

    /* ---------- 09 beam AB ---------- */
    const col = 1.5, lnE = A-col, lnM = B-col, lnA = (lnE+lnM)/2;
    const wdE = weq(DLslab,B,A), wlE = weq(LLo,B,A);
    const wdM = weq(DLslab,B,B), wlM = weq(LLo,B,B);
    const DE = wdE + fbStem + WALL, DM = wdM + fbStem + WALL;
    const wuE = 1.2*DE + 1.6*wlE, wuM = 1.2*DM + 1.6*wlM;
    const Ms = [wuE*lnE*lnE/16, wuE*lnE*lnE/14, wuE*lnA*lnA/10, wuM*lnM*lnM/16];
    const bw = 12, d = 17.5;
    const rmin = Math.max(3*Math.sqrt(fc*1000)/60000, 200/60000), Asmin = rmin*bw*d;

    set('abY',f1(Y[3])); set('abSpans',f1(A)+"&prime; &ndash; "+f1(B)+"&prime; &ndash; "+f1(A)+"&prime;");
    set('c2span',f1(A)+" vs "+f1(B)+" &rarr; "+f1(100*Math.abs(A-B)/Math.max(A,B))+"%");
    set('c2ld',f2(wlE/DE));
    set('c2panel',f1(B)+"&prime; &times; "+f1(A)+"&prime;");
    set('c2m',f3(A/B)); set('c2wd',f3(wdE)); set('c2wl',f3(wlE));
    set('c2D',f3(DE)); set('c2L',f3(wlE)); set('c2wu',f3(wuE)); set('c2wum',f3(wuM));
    set('c2lne',f2(lnE)); set('c2lnm',f2(lnM)); set('c2lna',f2(lnA));
    Ms.forEach((M,i)=>{
      const As = Math.max(AsReq(M,bw,d), Asmin);
      set('m'+(i+1), f1(M)); set('a'+(i+1), f2(As)); set('b'+(i+1), pick(As));
    });
    set('asmin',f2(Asmin)); set('ansM',r0(Ms[1])); set('ansM2',r0(Ms[2]));

    const phiVc = 0.75*2*Math.sqrt(fc*1000)*bw*d/1000;
    const Vu = 1.15*wuE*lnE/2, Vs = Math.max(0,(Vu-phiVc)/0.75);
    set('phiVc',f1(phiVc)); set('vumax',f1(Vu)); set('vsreq',f1(Vs));
    set('sMax', Math.min(Math.floor(d/2),24));

    /* ---------- 11 curtailment ---------- */
    const ld7 = (60000*1.3/(20*Math.sqrt(fc*1000)))*0.875/12;
    const ld6 = (60000*0.8/(25*Math.sqrt(fc*1000)))*0.75/12;
    const ldh7 = (60000/(55*Math.sqrt(fc*1000)))*Math.pow(0.875,1.5)/12;
    set('cut1',fi(lnE/4)); set('cut1b',fi(lnE/4));
    set('cut2',fi(lnM/4)); set('cut3',fi(lnE/5));
    set('ld7',fi(ld7)); set('ld7b',fi(ld7)); set('ld6',fi(ld6)); set('ldh7',fi(ldh7));

    /* ---------- 10 base shear ---------- */
    const Wslab = area*DLslab, Wbeam = (4*Lx + 4*Ly)*fbStem;
    const Wcol = 16*(270/144)*wc*C, Wwall = (2*(Lx+Ly) + 120)*WALL;
    const Wcore = 26*(10/12)*C*wc;
    const Wfl = Wslab+Wbeam+Wcol+Wwall+Wcore, W = N*Wfl;
    [['w1',Wslab],['w2',Wbeam],['w3',Wcol],['w4',Wwall],['w5',Wcore]].forEach(p=>set(p[0],f1(p[1])));
    set('w6',r0(Wfl)); set('w7',r0(W));
    set('wpsf',r0(Wfl/area*1000)+" psf"); set('wnst',N+" floors");
    set('s3area',r0(area)); set('wallTot',r0(N*Wwall));
    set('tbLo',r0(Math.round(W/100)*100));

    const hnm = hn*0.3048, S = 1.5, TB = 0.2, TC = 0.8, TD = 2.0, Z = 0.20, I = 1.0;
    function CsOf(T){
      if(T <= TB) return S*(1 + T/TB*1.5);
      if(T <= TC) return 2.5*S;
      if(T <= TD) return 2.5*S*(TC/T);
      return 2.5*S*(TC*TD/(T*T));
    }
    const Td = 0.0488*Math.pow(hnm,0.75), Tf = 0.0466*Math.pow(hnm,0.9);
    const Sa = (T,R) => (2/3)*(Z*I/R)*CsOf(T);
    const V55 = Sa(Td,5.5)*W, V70 = Sa(Td,7.0)*W, V55f = Sa(Tf,5.5)*W;
    set('s3hn',r0(hn)); set('s3hnm',f1(hnm)); set('s3T',f3(Td)); set('s3Cs',f3(CsOf(Td)));
    set('s3v1',r0(V55)+" kip"); set('s3v2',r0(V70)+" kip"); set('s3v3',r0(V55f)+" kip");
    set('vR55',r0(V55)+" k"); set('vR70',r0(V70)+" k");

    /* wind: integrate windward q(z) up the height, leeward at qh */
    const Vw = 147, Kd = 0.85, G = 0.85, alpha = 7.0, zg = 1200;
    const Kz = z => 2.01*Math.pow(Math.max(z,15)/zg, 2/alpha);
    const qh = 0.00256*Kz(hn)*Kd*Vw*Vw;
    function windV(width){
      let V = 0;
      for(let z = 0; z < hn; z += 1){
        const dz = Math.min(1, hn-z), qw = 0.00256*Kz(z+dz/2)*Kd*Vw*Vw;
        V += G*(0.8*qw + 0.5*qh)*width*dz/1000;
      }
      return V;
    }
    const Wx = windV(Ly), Wy = windV(Lx);
    set('s3fx',r0(Ly)); set('s3fy',r0(Lx));
    set('s3wx',r0(Wx)+" kip"); set('s3wy',r0(Wy)+" kip");
    const vLo = Math.min(V55,V70,V55f), vHi = Math.max(V55,V70,V55f), wMax = Math.max(Wx,Wy);
    set('s3lo',r0(vLo)); set('s3hi',r0(vHi)); set('s3wmax',r0(wMax));
    set('s3band',f1(100*vLo/W)+"&ndash;"+f1(100*vHi/W)+"%");
    set('s3windcmp', wMax < vLo ? "smaller but not negligible"
      : wMax > vHi ? "actually governing here — check both" : "overlapping the seismic range");

    /* ---------- quick reference ---------- */
    set('q1',r0(Lx)+"&prime; &times; "+r0(Ly)+"&prime;"); set('q2',fi(hn));
    set('q3',r0(W)+" kip"); set('q4',f1(AT)+" ft&sup2;");
    set('q5',r0(Math.round(Pu1/10)*10)+"&ndash;"+r0(Math.round(Pu2/10)*10)+" kip");
    set('q6',f1(Ms[1])+" k-ft"); set('q7',f1(Ms[2])+" k-ft");
    set('q8',r0(vLo)+"&ndash;"+r0(vHi)+" kip");
    set('q9',r0(Math.min(Wx,Wy))+"&ndash;"+r0(wMax)+" kip");

    drawPlan(X,Y); drawBeam(A,B,lnE,lnM);
  }

  /* ---------------- framing plan ---------------- */
  function drawPlan(X,Y){
    const svg = g('planSvg'); if(!svg) return;
    const W = 460, H = 420, mL = 52, mR = 30, mT = 30, mB = 70;
    const Lx = X[3], Ly = Y[3];
    const s = Math.min((W-mL-mR)/Lx, (H-mT-mB)/Ly);
    const px = x => mL + x*s, py = y => H - mB - y*s;
    const XL = ['1','2','3','4'], YL = ['A','B','C','D'];
    let o = '';
    Y.forEach(y => o += `<line x1="${px(0)}" y1="${py(y)}" x2="${px(Lx)}" y2="${py(y)}" stroke="#2e3548" stroke-width="1"/>`);
    X.forEach(x => o += `<line x1="${px(x)}" y1="${py(0)}" x2="${px(x)}" y2="${py(Ly)}" stroke="#2e3548" stroke-width="1"/>`);
    /* Beam AB along the top grid line */
    o += `<line x1="${px(0)}" y1="${py(Ly)}" x2="${px(Lx)}" y2="${py(Ly)}" stroke="#9b7fe8" stroke-width="3"/>`;
    o += `<text x="${px(Lx/2)}" y="${py(Ly)-9}" fill="#9b7fe8" font-size="11" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">BEAM AB</text>`;
    X.forEach((x,i)=>{
      o += `<circle cx="${px(x)}" cy="${py(0)+34}" r="10" fill="none" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1"/>`;
      o += `<text x="${px(x)}" y="${py(0)+38}" fill="#6b7280" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">${XL[i]}</text>`;
    });
    Y.forEach((y,i)=>{
      o += `<circle cx="${px(0)-32}" cy="${py(y)}" r="10" fill="none" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="1"/>`;
      o += `<text x="${px(0)-32}" y="${py(y)+4}" fill="#6b7280" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">${YL[i]}</text>`;
    });
    /* bay dimensions */
    for(let i=0;i<3;i++){
      o += `<text x="${px((X[i]+X[i+1])/2)}" y="${py(0)+17}" fill="#c8cede" font-size="9.5" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">${(X[i+1]-X[i]).toFixed(0)}'</text>`;
      o += `<text x="${px(0)-15}" y="${py((Y[i]+Y[i+1])/2)+3}" fill="#c8cede" font-size="9.5" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">${(Y[i+1]-Y[i]).toFixed(0)}'</text>`;
    }
    /* columns */
    X.forEach(x => Y.forEach(y => {
      const hot = (x===X[1] && y===Y[1]);
      o += `<rect x="${px(x)-5}" y="${py(y)-5}" width="10" height="10" rx="1.5"
        fill="${hot?'#d4a94a':'#4ec9b0'}" stroke="${hot?'#d4a94a':'#4ec9b0'}"
        stroke-width="${hot?3:1}" opacity="${hot?1:0.85}"/>`;
    }));
    o += `<circle cx="${px(X[1])}" cy="${py(Y[1])}" r="14" fill="none" stroke="#d4a94a" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/>`;
    o += `<text x="${px(X[1])-22}" y="${py(Y[1])+21}" fill="#d4a94a" font-size="10" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="end">C3 — circled</text>`;
    o += `<text x="${W/2}" y="${H-8}" fill="#6b7280" font-size="9.5" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">C1/C2/C3 assignment: read off your plan</text>`;
    svg.innerHTML = o;
  }

  /* ---------------- beam AB long section ---------------- */
  function drawBeam(A,B,lnE,lnM){
    const svg = g('beamSvg'); if(!svg) return;
    const W = 640, H = 250, mL = 26, mR = 26, yTop = 100, hBeam = 46;
    const total = A + B + A, s = (W-mL-mR)/total;
    const xs = [0, A, A+B, total].map(v => mL + v*s);
    const yB = yTop + hBeam, cw = 10;
    let o = '';
    /* columns at each support */
    xs.forEach(x => o += `<rect x="${x-cw/2}" y="${yTop-8}" width="${cw}" height="${hBeam+42}" fill="#1a1e2a" stroke="#2e3548" stroke-width="1"/>`);
    /* stirrups first, so the bars read on top of them */
    for(let x = xs[0]+10; x < xs[3]-6; x += 19)
      o += `<line x1="${x}" y1="${yTop+4}" x2="${x}" y2="${yB-4}" stroke="#5cb85c" stroke-width="0.8" opacity="0.35"/>`;
    /* beam body */
    o += `<rect x="${xs[0]}" y="${yTop}" width="${xs[3]-xs[0]}" height="${hBeam}" fill="none" stroke="#6ab0e0" stroke-width="1.6"/>`;
    const seg = (x1,x2,y,col) => `<line x1="${x1}" y1="${y}" x2="${x2}" y2="${y}" stroke="${col}" stroke-width="3.4" stroke-linecap="round"/>`;
    const yT = yTop + 8, yBt = yB - 8;
    /* top bars: ln/5 at the exterior supports, ln/4 each side of the interior ones */
    o += seg(xs[0], xs[0] + (lnE/5)*s, yT, '#c25a3b');
    o += seg(xs[3] - (lnE/5)*s, xs[3], yT, '#c25a3b');
    [1,2].forEach(i => o += seg(xs[i] - (lnE/4)*s, xs[i] + (lnM/4)*s, yT, '#c25a3b'));
    /* bottom steel runs through */
    o += seg(xs[0]+3, xs[3]-3, yBt, '#4ec9b0');
    /* dimension line */
    const yd = yB + 48;
    o += `<line x1="${xs[0]}" y1="${yd}" x2="${xs[3]}" y2="${yd}" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="0.8"/>`;
    xs.forEach(x => o += `<line x1="${x}" y1="${yd-5}" x2="${x}" y2="${yd+5}" stroke="#6b7280" stroke-width="0.8"/>`);
    [[0,A],[1,B],[2,A]].forEach(p => {
      const mid = (xs[p[0]] + xs[p[0]+1])/2;
      o += `<text x="${mid}" y="${yd+17}" fill="#c8cede" font-size="11" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="middle">${p[1].toFixed(0)}'-0"</text>`;
    });
    const ann = (x,y,t,col,anch) => `<text x="${x}" y="${y}" fill="${col}" font-size="9.5" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="${anch||'middle'}">${t}</text>`;
    /* cut-off callouts, one per distinct rule */
    o += ann(xs[0] + (lnE/5)*s/2, yT-10, `ln/5 = ${(lnE/5).toFixed(1)}'`, '#c25a3b');
    o += ann(xs[1], yT-10, `ln/4 = ${(lnE/4).toFixed(1)}' each side of support`, '#c25a3b');
    o += ann(xs[2], yBt+18, `bottom steel continuous through supports`, '#4ec9b0');
    /* legend */
    o += `<text x="${mL}" y="26" fill="#e0e4ef" font-size="11" font-family="monospace">FB 12×20 — three spans continuous</text>`;
    o += `<text x="${mL}" y="44" fill="#6b7280" font-size="9.5" font-family="monospace">red = top bars · teal = bottom bars · green = stirrups</text>`;
    o += `<text x="${W-mR}" y="26" fill="#6b7280" font-size="9.5" font-family="monospace" text-anchor="end">cut-offs measured from support face</text>`;
    svg.innerHTML = o;
  }

  ['pA','pB','pC','pN'].forEach(id => {
    const e = g(id); if(e){ e.addEventListener('input', recalc); e.addEventListener('change', recalc); }
  });
  recalc();
})();
</script>

</Base>
